<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="Srbinko Drakšan’s Favorit F11 followed him from a Belgrade cycling club to a careful restoration more than six decades later."/><link rel="canonical" href="https://teob.dk/en/stories/favorit-f11-en-cykel-og-en-ungdom/"/><link rel="alternate" hreflang="da" href="https://teob.dk/historier/favorit-f11-en-cykel-og-en-ungdom/"/><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://teob.dk/en/stories/favorit-f11-en-cykel-og-en-ungdom/"/><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://teob.dk/historier/favorit-f11-en-cykel-og-en-ungdom/"/><meta property="og:type" content="article"/><meta property="og:title" content="When the bicycle left its first owner · The Evolution of Bikes"/><meta property="og:description" content="A Favorit F11, a 1960 receipt and the photographs that preserved its connection to the first owner."/><meta property="og:image" content={hero.src}/><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"/><title>When the bicycle left its first owner · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="stories" danishPath="/historier/favorit-f11-en-cykel-og-en-ungdom/"/>
<main><article class="favorit-story"><header class="story-hero"><p class="section-label">Readers’ stories · Serbia · 1959–2026</p><h1>When the bicycle left<br/>its first <em>owner</em></h1><p>A gold Favorit, an old receipt and a meeting with the man who had kept the bicycle for more than six decades.</p></header>
<figure class="story-image hero"><OptimizedImage src={hero} alt="A young man riding a racing bicycle in an old black-and-white photograph" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high"/><figcaption>A photograph from Srbinko Drakšan’s youth. The family associates it with his years as a racing cyclist. Private archive.</figcaption></figure>
<aside class="favorit-contributor" aria-label="About the contributor" style="box-sizing:border-box;max-width:780px;margin:2.5rem auto;padding:1.4rem 1.6rem;border-left:4px solid #9c2f22;background:#eee9de;line-height:1.55"><p class="section-label">Story submitted by</p><p><strong>Stefan Đurić · Numenor Cycles</strong></p><p>Follow his restorations on <a href="https://www.facebook.com/profile.php?id=100095668024101" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><strong>Facebook</strong></a> and under <strong>#NumenorCycles</strong>.</p></aside>
<section class="story-copy"><p class="lead">After a two-hour drive to Pančevo, Stefan Đurić found himself in a living room where coffee and conversation arrived well before the bicycle.</p><p>A friend with a passion for vintage cars and Harley-Davidson motorcycles had sent him the advert. Everyone around Stefan knew his weakness for old racing bicycles, and the gold Favorit was difficult to ignore. He called and set off for the town near Belgrade with a friend for company.</p><p>The family welcomed them as if they had met before. Only after coffee, food and a quarter of an hour’s conversation did they begin to discuss the bicycle. It had belonged to the oldest man in the household, Srbinko Drakšan, and had been bought new in Pančevo.</p><p>Then the papers appeared: a receipt dated 1 December 1960 for 36,500 Yugoslav dinars and a membership card from the Avala cycling club in Belgrade, where Srbinko had trained and raced. Only the original pump had disappeared over the years.</p><p>Finally, Srbinko himself entered the room. He was 93. Age and dementia had taken much of his memory, and he did not recognise the bicycle. The large family needed the space, so it had to move on.</p></section>
<figure class="story-image portrait"><OptimizedImage src={portrait} alt="Stefan Đurić sitting beside the elderly Srbinko Drakšan"/><figcaption>Stefan Đurić and Srbinko Drakšan during the meeting in Pančevo. Private photograph.</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="story-image wide"><OptimizedImage src={found} alt="The gold Favorit racing bicycle before restoration"/><figcaption>The bicycle as Stefan found it in Pančevo: dusty, complete and stationary for many years.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="story-copy"><h2>A number beneath the bottom bracket</h2><p>On the drive home, the pleasure of the find sat beside a more difficult thought: after more than 60 years, he had separated the bicycle from its first owner. He promised to send the family photographs when the work was finished.</p><p>Favorit had been common in the former Yugoslavia, but Stefan had never worked closely with the marque. Old catalogues did not provide a firm model identification, so he contacted Marek Sučanek of OnRoad Vintage Bikes. Sučanek referred the question to Favorit specialist Milan Moravec.</p><p>The frame number beneath the bottom bracket was <strong>59F18033</strong>. Moravec identified the bicycle as an F11 and interpreted the number as pointing to 1959. The receipt therefore records its sale the following year.</p></section>
<div class="story-pair"><figure><OptimizedImage src={receipt} alt="Original receipt dated 1 December 1960 for a Favorit racing bicycle"/><figcaption>The receipt records 36,500 Yugoslav dinars and frame number 18033.</figcaption></figure><figure><OptimizedImage src={serial} alt="Frame number 59F18033 stamped beneath the Favorit bicycle’s bottom bracket"/><figcaption>The frame number beneath the bottom bracket.</figcaption></figure></div>
<figure class="story-image wide"><OptimizedImage src={components} alt="The Favorit bicycle’s dismantled parts laid out during restoration"/><figcaption>The parts were dismantled, cleaned, polished and lubricated before the bicycle was rebuilt.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="story-copy"><h2>The strip of red beneath the lever</h2><p>At the Numenor Cycles workshop, every component was dismantled, ultrasonically cleaned, polished, greased and reassembled. The frame retained its original gold finish. The deteriorated transfers disappeared during cleaning and were replaced with reproductions; according to Stefan, the remaining equipment was original to the bicycle.</p><p>The handlebar tape supplied the sort of small clue on which a restoration can turn. Yellow electrical tape covered pale-blue cotton tape. Milan Moravec explained that bicycles of the period could be delivered with red or grey tape.</p><p>When the blue tape came away from the brake lever clamp, a small strip of red remained underneath. That settled the question. Stefan even had a roll in the same shade and texture. Wine corks closed the bar ends, as riders often did at the time.</p><p>Only the bearing balls in the hubs and bottom bracket and the brake and gear cables were replaced. New red Christophe Zefal toe straps were fitted. White cable housing replaced the worn gold housing, but the original pieces were preserved with the papers.</p><p>Stefan does not intend to keep the F11. A teacher’s salary and limited workshop space place a natural limit on the number of projects he can retain. Its next owner will therefore receive not just the bicycle but its receipt and story as well.</p><p>Few old bicycles arrive with a receipt, club card and photographs of their first owner. This one did. Shortly after finishing the account, Stefan realised that the same day was Srbinko’s 94th birthday.</p></section>
<figure class="story-image wide"><OptimizedImage src={restored} alt="Restored gold Favorit F11 from 1959 seen from the drive side"/><figcaption>The completed Favorit F11. The frame retains its original gold finish; the transfers are reproductions. Photograph: Stefan Đurić.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="archive-gallery"><div class="gallery-heading"><p class="section-label">Papers, people and mechanics</p><h2>What accompanied the bicycle</h2><p>Selected photographs from the family and the restoration.</p></div><div class="gallery-grid">{gallery.map(([image, caption, alt]) => <figure><OptimizedImage src={image} alt={alt}/><figcaption>{caption}</figcaption></figure>)}</div></section>
<section class="period-sources"><p class="section-label">Sources and image note</p><div><p>The story is based on Stefan Đurić’s account, family photographs, the purchase receipt, club card and frame number. The model and year identification is Milan Moravec’s assessment.</p><p>Text and images were submitted by Stefan Đurić. Follow his restoration work at <a href="https://www.facebook.com/profile.php?id=100095668024101" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Numenor Cycles on Facebook</a> and under <strong>#NumenorCycles</strong>.</p></div></section>
